---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Pub from '../components/Pub.astro';
// Copy is Charlie's own pitch for the book (October 2026), in the style of a publisher's description.
const pubs = await getCollection('publications');
const coverage = new Map((await getCollection('coverage')).map((c) => [c.id, c.data]));
// Papers from the book, in the order the pitch reaches them: voters, spending, legitimacy.
const BOOK_PAPERS = ['too-young-to-run-2024', 'social-spending-2026', 'legitimacy-2026'];
const papers = BOOK_PAPERS.map((id) => pubs.find((p) => p.id === id)).filter((p) => p !== undefined);
// Award pill and PDF fallback come from coverage.yaml; the per-paper media list is dropped here
// because the Coverage section below lists the book's press as a whole.
const extraFor = (id: string) => {
  const c = coverage.get(id);
  return c ? { ...c, items: [] } : undefined;
};
---

<Base title="Silver Democracy" description="Silver Democracy: Youth Representation in an Aging Japan. Why young people are underrepresented in elected office, and what changes when they win.">
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker">Book project</p>
    <h1>Silver Democracy <span class="sub">Youth Representation in an Aging Japan</span></h1>
    <p class="questions">
      Why are young people underrepresented in elected office? And what changes for policy and democracy when
      they win?
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      In nearly every democracy, the people who will live longest with the consequences of today’s decisions are
      the very people least likely to be making them. As populations age and the young become an ever smaller
      proportion of the electorate, the worry is that their concerns will be even less represented and their faith
      in democratic institutions will continue to erode. Japan offers a window into that future. It is the world’s
      oldest society and one where the young are especially underrepresented at all levels of government, from
      local assemblies to the national parliament.
    </p>
    <p>
      In <em>Silver Democracy</em>, I argue that the problem is not that young candidates are less appealing to
      voters. To the contrary, many voters, including older voters, like young candidates for their fresh ideas,
      their focus on the needs of young families, and their personal stake in the long-term challenges of population
      aging and decline. The problem is institutional. In Japan, as in many democracies, a successful candidacy often depends
      on money, name recognition, and networks that can take decades to build. These demands favor older
      people with established careers, keeping younger people off the ballot before voters get a say.
    </p>
    <p>
      I then show how youth representation has substantial consequences for policy and democracy. Municipalities
      that elect a young mayor increase spending on child welfare for young families, especially through long-term
      investments in childcare infrastructure, but do not cut elderly welfare spending. Bringing more young people
      into municipal assemblies also enhances perceptions of fairness and trust in institutions, even among the
      elderly. Together, these findings suggest that greater youth representation can encourage investment in the
      future and strengthen confidence in democratic institutions without pitting young against old.
    </p>
    <p>
      The book draws on both quantitative and qualitative methods. At the heart is my newly constructed{' '}
      <a href="/jmed/">Japanese Municipal Elections Dataset (JMED)</a>, which covers the near universe of
      candidates who ran for municipal assembly or mayor from 2005 to 2025: 223,569 candidates competing in 21,111
      elections across 2,027 municipalities. With this new data, I first use quasi-experimental analyses to examine
      how institutions keep young people off the ballot and how policy changes when young people are elected. I
      then pair these observational tests with survey experiments that use age regression and progression software
      to vary politicians’ apparent ages and see how citizens respond, and with evidence from other advanced
      democracies to show that the results extend beyond Japan.
    </p>
    <p>
      Finally, I draw on more than 40 interviews in Japanese with mayors, assembly members, and local officials to
      understand how younger politicians decide to run and what their priorities are once in office.
    </p>

    <h2>Related papers</h2>
    <ul class="works">
      {papers.map((p) => <Pub pub={p.data} extra={extraFor(p.id)} />)}
    </ul>

    <h2>Coverage</h2>
    <p>
      I have discussed the book's findings in the{' '}
      <a href="https://www.asahi.com/articles/DA3S15557764.html">Asahi Shimbun</a>,{' '}
      <a href="https://www.economist.com/asia/2024/06/27/takashima-ryosuke-is-japans-youngest-ever-mayor">The Economist</a>,{' '}
      <a href="https://www.npr.org/2023/06/28/1184894573/the-growing-concern-of-japans-silver-democracy">NPR</a>{' '}
      (<a href="https://www.npr.org/2023/06/29/1184972678/rapidly-aging-societies-such-as-japan-worry-about-whats-called-silver-democracy">2</a>), and{' '}
      <a href="https://www.wsj.com/video/series/wsj-explains/why-the-world-leaders-are-getting-older/7038C1C2-4CDC-47E3-8F3C-AD5F37235C5E">The Wall Street Journal</a>{' '}
      (<a href="https://www.wsj.com/world/for-world-leaders-70-is-the-new-50-290c1ba7">2</a>).
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .sub { display: block; font-size: 0.55em; color: var(--ink-2); font-weight: 400; margin-top: 0.4rem; letter-spacing: 0; }
  .questions { margin: 1.4rem 0 0; max-width: var(--measure); font-size: 1.25rem; line-height: 1.4; color: var(--ink); }
</style>
